{"name":"Time Travel","hex":"#b3c4d5","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"time travel","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Travel to the past or to the future, typically by means of a time machine or a wormhole.","The ability to access previous states of a database."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Alternative form of time-travel"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/time_travel"}],"entries":[{"title":"Time Travel (Alessi's Ark album)","blocks":[{"text":"Time Travel is the second album by British singer-songwriter Alessi Laurent-Marke and her first album for independent label Bella Union. It was recorded in Brighton's Electric Studios with Willkommen Collective’s Marcus Hamblett and at Bryn Derwen in Wales with David Wrench."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/6/63/TimeTravel-cover-AlessisArk.jpg/330px-TimeTravel-cover-AlessisArk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_Travel_(Alessi's_Ark_album)"},{"title":"Time travel","blocks":[{"text":"Time travel is the hypothetical activity of traveling into the past or future. Time travel is a concept in philosophy, space, time and fiction, particularly science fiction. In fiction, time travel is typically achieved through the use of a device known as a time machine. The idea of a time machine was popularized by H. G. Wells's 1895 novel The Time Machine."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/The_Time_Machine_%E2%80%93_Frontpage_Heinemann.png/330px-The_Time_Machine_%E2%80%93_Frontpage_Heinemann.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_travel"},{"title":"Time","blocks":[{"text":"Time is the continuous progression of existence that occurs in an apparently irreversible succession from the past, through the present, and into the future. Time dictates all forms of action, age, and causality, being a component quantity of various measurements used to sequence events, to compare the duration of events or the intervals between them, and to quantify rates of change of quantities in material reality or in conscious experience. Time is often referred to as the fourth dimension and the temporal dimension, in addition to the three spatial dimensions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/MontreGousset001.jpg/330px-MontreGousset001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/6/63/TimeTravel-cover-AlessisArk.jpg/330px-TimeTravel-cover-AlessisArk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/6/63/TimeTravel-cover-AlessisArk.jpg/330px-TimeTravel-cover-AlessisArk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATimeTravel-cover-AlessisArk.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/The_Time_Machine_%E2%80%93_Frontpage_Heinemann.png/500px-The_Time_Machine_%E2%80%93_Frontpage_Heinemann.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/The_Time_Machine_%E2%80%93_Frontpage_Heinemann.png/1280px-The_Time_Machine_%E2%80%93_Frontpage_Heinemann.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/The_Time_Machine_%E2%80%93_Frontpage_Heinemann.png/500px-The_Time_Machine_%E2%80%93_Frontpage_Heinemann.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The first page of The Time Machine published by Heinemann","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_Time_Machine_%E2%80%93_Frontpage_Heinemann.png"},{"srcs